<main>
    <section class="wm-hero wm-study-hero">
        <div class="wm-study-shell">
            <p class="wm-study-kicker">The study that explains why recomposition matters</p>
            <h1 class="wm-study-title">Don't just lose weight. <em>Change what your body is made of.</em></h1>
            <p class="wm-study-lede">
                Retaining muscle doesn't just help you look, feel, and perform better. Lean mass and strength are important markers of healthy aging, and low lean mass is associated with higher mortality risk. This 1999 randomized trial shows why we <strong>RECOMPOSE OUR BODIES</strong>, not just lose weight.
            </p>
            <p class="wm-study-question">All three groups lost about 20–22 lb. Look at what that weight was made of. Don't you want the third result?</p>

            <div class="wm-comparison" aria-label="Kraemer 1999 comparison of diet alone, diet plus cardio, and diet plus cardio plus resistance training">
                <div class="wm-comparison-head" aria-hidden="true">
                    <div>Approach</div>
                    <div>12-week result</div>
                    <div>What it means</div>
                </div>

                <article class="wm-comparison-row is-diet">
                    <div class="wm-result-image">
                        <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/4051009/pexels-photo-4051009.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="A small healthy meal portion on a plate" loading="eager" />
                        <div class="wm-result-label">Diet Alone</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="wm-result-numbers">
                        <div class="wm-total-loss"><span>Total weight lost</span>21.3 lb</div>
                        <div class="wm-tissue-split">
                            <div class="wm-fat"><b>14.7 lb</b><span>estimated fat lost</span></div>
                            <div class="wm-lean"><b>6.6 lb</b><span>estimated fat-free tissue lost*</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="wm-effect">
                        <strong>The scale went down—but nearly one-third of the loss was fat-free tissue.</strong>
                        <p>Dieting creates the energy deficit, but without an exercise stimulus this group significantly lost fat-free mass and also showed a decline in peak and mean power.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="wm-comparison-row is-cardio">
                    <div class="wm-result-image">
                        <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/6455836/pexels-photo-6455836.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="A person walking on a treadmill in a modern gym" loading="lazy" />
                        <div class="wm-result-label">Diet + Cardio</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="wm-result-numbers">
                        <div class="wm-total-loss"><span>Total weight lost</span>19.8 lb</div>
                        <div class="wm-tissue-split">
                            <div class="wm-fat"><b>15.5 lb</b><span>estimated fat lost</span></div>
                            <div class="wm-lean"><b>4.4 lb</b><span>estimated fat-free tissue lost*</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="wm-effect">
                        <strong>Cardio improved aerobic fitness, but it did not protect lean tissue nearly as well as adding resistance training.</strong>
                        <p>This group improved peak oxygen consumption substantially, yet about 22% of its weight loss was still fat-free tissue.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="wm-comparison-row is-recomp">
                    <div class="wm-result-image">
                        <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/9958673/pexels-photo-9958673.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="A group of people strength training with weights in a gym" loading="lazy" />
                        <div class="wm-result-label">Recomp</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="wm-result-numbers">
                        <div class="wm-total-loss"><span>Total weight lost</span>21.8 lb</div>
                        <div class="wm-tissue-split">
                            <div class="wm-fat"><b>21.2 lb</b><span>estimated fat lost</span></div>
                            <div class="wm-lean"><b>0.7 lb</b><span>estimated fat-free tissue lost*</span></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="wm-effect">
                        <strong>Almost all of the weight lost was fat—while strength increased.</strong>
                        <p>Diet + aerobic exercise + resistance training produced about 97% of weight loss from fat, while bench-press strength rose 19.6% and squat strength rose 32.6%. This is the result 12 Week Recomp is designed around.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="wm-study-note">
                <span>Kraemer et al., 1999 • 35 overweight men • 12-week randomized trial.</span>
                <span>*The study measured fat-free mass, not skeletal muscle alone. Pound values are rounded estimates calculated from the published total weight loss and published fat-loss percentages.</span>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10487375/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original study ↗</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wm-section wm-summary" id="summary">
        <div class="wm-section-heading">
            <p>THE CONCLUSION IN PLAIN ENGLISH</p>
            <h2>Losing weight is not the same as improving your body.</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="wm-prose">
            <p>Most diets are judged by one number: how far the scale goes down. But the scale cannot tell you whether you lost fat, lean tissue, water, or some combination of all three. That distinction is the entire reason recomposition matters.</p>

            <p>In one 12-week randomized trial, the diet-only group, the diet-plus-cardio group, and the diet-plus-cardio-plus-resistance group all lost almost the same amount of total body weight—roughly 9 to 10 kilograms. If you looked only at the scale, you could easily call all three approaches equally successful.</p>

            <p>They were not.</p>

            <p><strong>Diet alone:</strong> about <strong>69% of the weight lost was fat</strong>. That means roughly <strong>31% of the weight lost was fat-free tissue</strong>.</p>

            <p><strong>Diet plus aerobic exercise:</strong> about <strong>78% of the weight lost was fat</strong>. Roughly <strong>22% was fat-free tissue</strong>.</p>

            <p><strong>Diet plus aerobic exercise plus resistance training:</strong> about <strong>97% of the weight lost was fat</strong>. Only about <strong>3% was fat-free tissue</strong>.</p>

            <p class="wm-punch">Same basic scale result. Completely different tissue result. The resistance-training group directed almost all of its weight loss toward fat while protecting nearly all of its lean tissue.</p>

            <p>And the resistance-training group did not merely preserve tissue. Strength improved substantially: bench press strength increased about <strong>19.6%</strong> and squat strength about <strong>32.6%</strong>. In other words, those subjects became lighter while becoming stronger.</p>

            <p>That is why 12 Week Recomp does not define success as “lose as much weight as possible.” A smaller number on the scale is not automatically a healthier outcome if a large part of the loss came from the lean tissue that supports strength, mobility, glucose control, physical function, and long-term independence.</p>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="wm-closing-quote">
            <span>The goal is not simply to lose weight.</span>
            <strong>The goal is to lose fat while protecting—or building—the body underneath it.</strong>
        </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section class="wm-section wm-decision" id="true-recomp">
        <div class="wm-section-heading">
            <p>AND IT CAN GO EVEN FURTHER</p>
            <h2>True recomposition: fat down while lean mass goes up</h2>
            <span>Preserving lean tissue during weight loss is valuable. But some controlled studies have gone further: participants lost body fat while simultaneously gaining lean mass. That is true body recomposition.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="wm-feature-grid">
            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">RANDOMIZED • 4 WEEKS • 40% ENERGY DEFICIT</div>
                <h3>Longland et al., 2016</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">High protein + intense resistance and interval training during a large calorie deficit</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>−4.8 kg</b><span>fat mass</span></div>
                    <div><b>+1.2 kg</b><span>lean body mass</span></div>
                    <div><b>2.4 g/kg</b><span>daily protein</span></div>
                    <div><b>40%</b><span>energy deficit</span></div>
                </div>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>What happened:</b> body fat dropped dramatically while lean body mass increased. The protocol was intentionally aggressive and is not a normal expected rate, but it demonstrates that fat loss and lean-mass gain can occur at the same time.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26817506/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 26817506 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">RANDOMIZED • 10 WEEKS • RESISTANCE TRAINED • 2026</div>
                <h3>Vargas-Molina et al., 2026</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">High-protein resistance training at maintenance calories or a moderate calorie deficit</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>−2.94 kg</b><span>fat • moderate deficit</span></div>
                    <div><b>+1.04 kg</b><span>fat-free mass • moderate deficit</span></div>
                    <div><b>−1.41 kg</b><span>fat • isocaloric</span></div>
                    <div><b>+0.97 kg</b><span>fat-free mass • isocaloric</span></div>
                </div>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>What happened:</b> both supervised groups gained fat-free mass while reducing fat. The moderate-deficit group lost more fat without preventing lean-mass gain.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41940947/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 41940947 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">8 WEEKS • TRAINED WOMEN</div>
                <h3>Campbell et al., 2018</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">Higher protein during a resistance-training program</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>−1.1 kg</b><span>fat mass</span></div>
                    <div><b>+2.1 kg</b><span>fat-free mass</span></div>
                    <div><b>2.5 g/kg</b><span>daily protein</span></div>
                    <div><b>8 weeks</b><span>resistance training</span></div>
                </div>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>What happened:</b> already training-oriented women added fat-free mass while losing fat without a prescribed crash diet.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29405780/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 29405780 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">8 WEEKS • N=48 • TRAINED ADULTS</div>
                <h3>Antonio et al., 2015</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">High-protein diet during resistance training</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>−1.7 kg</b><span>fat mass</span></div>
                    <div><b>+1.5 kg</b><span>fat-free mass</span></div>
                    <div><b>−0.1 kg</b><span>scale weight</span></div>
                    <div><b>3.4 g/kg</b><span>daily protein</span></div>
                </div>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>What happened:</b> the scale barely moved, yet the body changed substantially—fat went down and fat-free mass went up. This is exactly why scale weight alone can miss recomposition.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26500462/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 26500462 ↗</a>
            </article>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="wm-closing-quote">
            <span>This is the difference 12 Week Recomp is built around.</span>
            <strong>Fat down. Lean tissue protected—or increased. Strength and fitness moving forward.</strong>
        </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section class="wm-section wm-decision" id="decision-studies">
        <div class="wm-section-heading">
            <p>THE MOST DIRECT WEIGHT-LOSS EVIDENCE</p>
            <h2>When the scale falls, what happens to the rest of you?</h2>
            <span>These trials show why body composition, strength, and function matter more than scale weight by itself.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="wm-feature-grid">
            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">RANDOMIZED • 12 WEEKS • N=35</div>
                <h3>Kraemer et al., 1999</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">Diet vs. diet + aerobic vs. diet + aerobic + resistance training</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>−9.64 kg</b><span>diet weight loss</span></div>
                    <div><b>−8.99 kg</b><span>diet + aerobic</span></div>
                    <div><b>−9.90 kg</b><span>diet + aerobic + resistance</span></div>
                    <div><b>97%</b><span>fat share of loss with resistance</span></div>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Composition was the difference:</strong> fat accounted for about 69%, 78%, and 97% of the weight lost, respectively. The combined aerobic + resistance group increased bench press strength 19.6% and squat strength 32.6%; peak VO₂ rose 15.4% in that group and 24.8% with aerobic training.</p>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>Why it matters:</b> nearly identical scale loss can hide dramatically different tissue outcomes.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10487375/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 10487375 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">RANDOMIZED • 26 WEEKS • N=160</div>
                <h3>Villareal et al., 2017</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">Aerobic vs. resistance vs. combined exercise during weight loss in adults 65+</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>≈9%</b><span>body-weight loss in all exercise groups</span></div>
                    <div><b>−2.7 kg</b><span>lean mass • aerobic</span></div>
                    <div><b>−1.0 kg</b><span>lean mass • resistance</span></div>
                    <div><b>−1.7 kg</b><span>lean mass • combined</span></div>
                </div>
                <p>Fat mass fell about 6.3 kg with aerobic, 7.3 kg with resistance, and 7.0 kg with combined training. Strength improved about 4%, 19%, and 18%; peak VO₂ improved about 18%, 8%, and 17%, respectively. Combined training produced the strongest overall functional improvement.</p>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>Why it matters:</b> aerobic training excelled at aerobic fitness; resistance protected lean tissue and strength; combining them produced the broadest functional result.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28514618/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 28514618 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">CONTROLLED • 16 WEEKS • OLDER WOMEN</div>
                <h3>Campbell et al., 2009</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">500 kcal/day deficit with or without resistance training</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>−5.8 kg</b><span>body mass • sedentary</span></div>
                    <div><b>−5.0 kg</b><span>body mass • resistance</span></div>
                    <div><b>−1.6 kg</b><span>FFM • sedentary</span></div>
                    <div><b>−0.3 kg</b><span>FFM • resistance</span></div>
                </div>
                <p>Fat loss was similar (−4.1 vs. −4.7 kg), but fat-free mass decreased in the sedentary group and was statistically unchanged in the resistance-training group.</p>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>Why it matters:</b> resistance training changed what was preserved during similar diet-induced weight loss.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19247271/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 19247271 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-feature-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">RANDOMIZED • 6 MONTHS • FRAIL OLDER ADULTS</div>
                <h3>Frimel et al., 2008</h3>
                <p class="wm-study-name">Diet alone vs. diet + exercise including progressive resistance training</p>
                <div class="wm-big-stats">
                    <div><b>−10.7 kg</b><span>weight • diet</span></div>
                    <div><b>−9.7 kg</b><span>weight • diet + exercise</span></div>
                    <div><b>−3.5 kg</b><span>FFM • diet</span></div>
                    <div><b>−1.8 kg</b><span>FFM • diet + exercise</span></div>
                </div>
                <p>Fat loss was similar (−6.8 vs. −7.7 kg), while the exercise group lost about half as much fat-free mass and increased upper- and lower-body strength by 17–43%.</p>
                <p class="wm-study-take"><b>Why it matters:</b> similar weight and fat loss did not mean similar preservation of muscle-related tissue or strength.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18580399/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed • PMID 18580399 ↗</a>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wm-section" id="study-ledger">
        <div class="wm-section-heading">
            <p>BODY-COMPOSITION EVIDENCE LEDGER</p>
            <h2>The additional studies reviewed</h2>
            <span>Different populations and protocols produce different magnitudes. The repeated pattern is what matters: progressive resistance training and sufficient protein change what happens to lean tissue during fat loss—and can sometimes produce fat loss and lean gain at the same time.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="wm-study-grid">
            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">12 WEEKS • N=38</div>
                <h3>Demling &amp; DeSanti, 2000</h3>
                <p>All three groups lost about <b>2.5 kg body weight</b>. Diet alone lost <b>2.5 kg fat</b> with no lean gain. Diet + RT + 1.5 g/kg protein lost <b>7.0 kg fat / gained 4.0 kg lean</b> in the casein arm and lost <b>4.2 kg fat / gained 2.0 kg lean</b> in the whey arm. Strength increased 59% and 29% in those two training arms.</p>
                <small>Useful for the training + protein + deficit interaction; not evidence that one protein source is universally superior.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10838463/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">7 WEEKS • N=15 TRAINED WOMEN</div>
                <h3>Rauch et al., 2018</h3>
                <p>College volleyball players completing progressive full-body resistance training showed an overall time effect of roughly <b>+2.7 kg lean mass</b> and <b>−2.1 kg fat mass</b> across the training conditions.</p>
                <small>A short, athletic-population example of simultaneous lean gain and fat reduction without a prescribed large dieting phase.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30518071/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">ATHLETES • SLOW VS. FAST LOSS</div>
                <h3>Garthe et al., 2011</h3>
                <p>Slow loss averaged a <b>19% energy reduction</b> over 8.5 weeks; fast loss used about a <b>30% reduction</b> over 5.3 weeks. Both lost ~5.5% body weight. Fat mass fell <b>31% vs. 21%</b>, while lean body mass <b>increased 2.1%</b> with slower loss and was essentially unchanged (<b>−0.2%</b>) with faster loss.</p>
                <small>Supports the idea that a more moderate deficit can better coexist with training adaptation.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21558571/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">10 WEEKS • TRAINED MEN</div>
                <h3>Cribb et al., 2006</h3>
                <p>During supervised resistance training, the whey-isolate group reported <b>+5.0 kg lean mass</b> and <b>−1.5 kg fat mass</b>; the casein group reported <b>+0.8 kg lean</b> and <b>+0.2 kg fat</b>.</p>
                <small>Small study (13 recreational bodybuilders); useful as a trained-population recomp example, not as a basis for assuming those magnitudes in general users.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17240782/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">6 WEEKS • EXTREME-VOLUME RT</div>
                <h3>Haun et al., 2018</h3>
                <p>The graded-whey group showed <b>−1.00 kg fat mass</b> and <b>+2.93 kg DXA lean mass</b>; the maltodextrin group also gained <b>+2.35 kg DXA lean mass</b>. After correcting lean-mass change for extracellular water, gains were <b>+1.18 kg</b> from pre to midpoint and only <b>+0.25 kg</b> from midpoint to post (not significant).</p>
                <small>An important measurement caution: DXA “lean mass” can be influenced by fluid, especially during extreme-volume training.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30255024/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">4 WEEKS • N=27 TRAINED MEN</div>
                <h3>Kreipke et al., 2015</h3>
                <p>Both groups completed a progressive high-intensity resistance program while receiving either a multi-ingredient supplement or isocaloric placebo. The study measured DXA body composition and maximal strength over only four weeks.</p>
                <small>Reviewed as short-duration trained-population context. Because the PubMed abstract does not report the exact body-composition deltas, this page does not present unverified lean/fat change numbers.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26595135/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">2-WEEK DEFICIT • N=20 ATHLETES</div>
                <h3>Mettler et al., 2010</h3>
                <p>At 60% of habitual energy intake, the ~1.0 g/kg protein group lost <b>3.0 kg total</b> and <b>1.6 kg lean mass</b>. The ~2.3 g/kg group lost <b>1.5 kg total</b> and only <b>0.3 kg lean mass</b>. Lean loss differed significantly (p=.006).</p>
                <small>Shows why protein becomes especially important when energy restriction is aggressive.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19927027/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">8 WEEKS • TRAINED WOMEN</div>
                <h3>Campbell et al., 2018</h3>
                <p>With resistance training, the 2.5 g/kg protein group increased fat-free mass from 47.1 to 49.2 kg (<b>+2.1 kg</b>) and reduced fat mass from 14.1 to 13.0 kg (<b>−1.1 kg</b>). The 0.9 g/kg group gained <b>+0.6 kg FFM</b>; its fat change was not significant.</p>
                <small>Recomp occurred in already training-oriented women without a prescribed large calorie deficit.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29405780/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">8 WEEKS • N=48 TRAINED ADULTS</div>
                <h3>Antonio et al., 2015</h3>
                <p>The high-protein group averaged <b>3.4 g/kg/day</b>, lost <b>1.7 kg fat</b>, and gained <b>1.5 kg FFM</b> while body weight changed only −0.1 kg. The normal-protein group gained the same <b>1.5 kg FFM</b> but lost only 0.3 kg fat and gained 1.3 kg body weight.</p>
                <small>A useful demonstration of why body weight alone can miss meaningful recomposition.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26500462/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">7 WEEKS • ~25% DEFICIT • TRAINED ADULTS</div>
                <h3>Campbell et al., 2020</h3>
                <p>Both groups lost similar fat: <b>−2.8 kg</b> with two consecutive higher-carbohydrate refeed days and <b>−2.3 kg</b> with continuous restriction. FFM fell only <b>−0.4 kg</b> in the refeed group versus <b>−1.3 kg</b> with continuous restriction; dry FFM and resting metabolic rate were also better maintained.</p>
                <small>Supports periodized energy/carbohydrate intake as one possible strategy for preserving training adaptation during a deficit.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33467235/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">8 WEEKS • N=33 TRAINED MEN</div>
                <h3>Alcaraz et al., 2011</h3>
                <p>Both high-resistance circuit and traditional strength training increased <b>lean mass</b> and 1RM strength. Only the high-resistance circuit group showed a significant reduction in <b>body-fat percentage</b>; the circuit sessions used much shorter inter-set recovery.</p>
                <small>The published abstract does not provide exact kg changes, so the evidence is presented at the level the source supports.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21659889/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">8 WEEKS • FEMALE COLLEGE ATHLETES</div>
                <h3>Taylor et al., 2016</h3>
                <p>With four training days per week, the whey group gained <b>+1.4 kg lean mass</b> and lost <b>−1.0 kg fat</b>. The maltodextrin group gained +0.4 kg lean (trend) and lost −0.5 kg fat (not significant). Bench-press 1RM increased <b>+4.9 vs. +2.3 kg</b>.</p>
                <small>Another trained-female example of lean gain occurring alongside fat reduction.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26842665/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">1 YEAR • N=107 • AGE 65+</div>
                <h3>Villareal et al., 2011</h3>
                <p>Diet + exercise improved Physical Performance Test score <b>21%</b> versus 12% with diet and 15% with exercise alone. Peak VO₂ improved <b>17%</b> versus 10% and 8%. Diet and diet + exercise reduced body weight about 10% and 9%; lean mass fell <b>5%</b> with diet but only <b>3%</b> with diet + exercise.</p>
                <small>Weight loss plus exercise improved function more broadly while attenuating lean-mass loss.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21449785/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card wm-context-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">MECHANISTIC CONTEXT • 60 DAYS BED REST</div>
                <h3>Kramer et al., 2017</h3>
                <p>In the inactive bed-rest control, DXA lean mass fell from <b>56.9 to 53.0 kg (−3.9 kg)</b> while fat mass was essentially unchanged (16.9 to 17.0 kg). A brief high-intensity jump-training countermeasure substantially protected lean mass and leg power.</p>
                <small>Not a weight-loss study. Included to illustrate how rapidly lean tissue can be lost under extreme disuse.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28081223/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="wm-study-card wm-context-card">
                <div class="wm-study-meta">MECHANISTIC CONTEXT • 8 WEEKS OVERFEEDING</div>
                <h3>Bray et al., 2012</h3>
                <p>During controlled overeating, the low-protein group gained <b>3.16 kg body weight</b> but lost <b>0.70 kg lean mass</b>. Normal- and high-protein groups gained <b>2.87 and 3.18 kg lean mass</b>, respectively. Fat gain occurred across groups and did not differ materially by protein intake.</p>
                <small>Not a fat-loss trial. It demonstrates that scale weight and lean-tissue response can diverge dramatically depending on diet composition.</small>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22215165/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wm-section wm-health" id="health-evidence">
        <div class="wm-section-heading">
            <p>MUSCLE IS MORE THAN APPEARANCE</p>
            <h2>Why protecting muscle matters for health</h2>
            <span>These studies support the broader claim that muscle, strength, and resistance training matter for health and aging. Observational associations are identified as such and should not be read as proof that lifting alone caused the observed mortality differences.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="wm-health-grid">
            <article>
                <span class="wm-health-tag">LEAN MASS &amp; LONGEVITY • OBSERVATIONAL</span>
                <h3>Li et al., 2025 meta-analysis</h3>
                <p><b>11 prospective studies • 130,079 adults.</b> The lowest vs. normal lean-mass category was associated with a <b>30% higher all-cause mortality risk</b> (RR 1.30; 95% CI 1.16–1.47).</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40636391/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID 40636391 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article>
                <span class="wm-health-tag">RESISTANCE TRAINING &amp; MORTALITY • OBSERVATIONAL</span>
                <h3>Shailendra et al., 2022 meta-analysis</h3>
                <p><b>10 studies.</b> Any resistance training was associated with <b>15% lower all-cause mortality</b> (RR .85), <b>19% lower cardiovascular mortality</b> (RR .81), and <b>14% lower cancer mortality</b> (RR .86). The dose-response analysis found its largest association around 60 min/week.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35599175/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID 35599175 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article>
                <span class="wm-health-tag">BLOOD PRESSURE • RANDOMIZED-TRIAL META-ANALYSIS</span>
                <h3>Edwards et al., 2023</h3>
                <p><b>270 randomized trials • 15,827 participants.</b> Dynamic resistance training reduced resting blood pressure by about <b>4.55/3.04 mmHg</b>; aerobic training by 4.49/2.53; combined training by 6.04/2.54; and isometric training by 8.24/4.00.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37491419/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID 37491419 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article>
                <span class="wm-health-tag">VASCULAR FUNCTION • META-ANALYSIS</span>
                <h3>Silva et al., 2021</h3>
                <p><b>23 studies • 785 participants.</b> Resistance training improved brachial-artery flow-mediated dilation by <b>2.39 percentage points</b> (95% CI 1.65–3.14); dynamic resistance training alone improved it by 2.12 points.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34399984/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID 34399984 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article>
                <span class="wm-health-tag">OLDER ADULTS • QUALITY OF LIFE &amp; STRENGTH</span>
                <h3>Khodadad Kashi et al., 2023</h3>
                <p><b>21 studies • 1,610 adults age 60+.</b> Resistance training improved multiple quality-of-life domains and depression scores. Mean strength improvements included <b>+15.26 kg upper limb</b>, <b>+48.46 kg lower limb</b>, and <b>+1.35 kg handgrip</b> across the pooled analyses.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35968662/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID 35968662 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article>
                <span class="wm-health-tag">AGE 75+ • MUSCLE &amp; STRENGTH</span>
                <h3>Grgic et al., 2020</h3>
                <p><b>22 randomized studies.</b> Resistance training produced a large pooled effect on muscle strength (<b>ES 0.97</b>; 95% CI .50–1.44) and a significant effect on whole-muscle hypertrophy (<b>ES .30</b>). In adults 80+, the strength effect was ES 1.28.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32740889/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID 32740889 ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article>
                <span class="wm-health-tag">INSULIN RESISTANCE • TYPE 2 DIABETES</span>
                <h3>GREAT2DO trial, 2013</h3>
                <p><b>103 older adults • 12 months.</b> Within the progressive resistance-training group, improvement in insulin resistance tracked with increased skeletal muscle (<b>r=−.38</b>) and decreased fat mass (<b>r=.42</b>); changes in HbA1c were related to changes in muscle attenuation.</p>
                <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23474589/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID 23474589 ↗</a>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wm-section wm-interpretation">
        <div class="wm-section-heading">
            <p>WHAT THE EVIDENCE DOES — AND DOES NOT — SAY</p>
            <h2>Strong evidence does not require exaggerated claims</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="wm-rule-grid">
            <article><b>1</b><h3>“Fat-free tissue” is not identical to skeletal muscle.</h3><p>The 31%, 22%, and 3% comparison comes from fat versus fat-free mass. Fat-free mass includes skeletal muscle but also water and other non-fat tissue. Calling it all “muscle” would overstate the measurement.</p></article>
            <article><b>2</b><h3>True recomp has been measured.</h3><p>Longland, Vargas-Molina, Campbell, Antonio, Taylor, Rauch and other trials report conditions in which fat mass decreased while lean or fat-free mass increased.</p></article>
            <article><b>3</b><h3>Study populations matter.</h3><p>Older adults, athletes, overweight men, trained women, and people with diabetes do not respond identically. Results demonstrate patterns and possibilities, not guaranteed individual outcomes.</p></article>
            <article><b>4</b><h3>Cardio still matters.</h3><p>Aerobic training remains a powerful tool for cardiorespiratory fitness. The point is not “weights instead of cardio.” It is that a health-focused fat-loss program should not sacrifice the resistance stimulus that protects muscle and strength.</p></article>
            <article><b>5</b><h3>Mortality evidence is association, not a lifting prescription.</h3><p>Prospective cohorts can show that low lean mass or resistance-training participation tracks with mortality risk, but they cannot prove causality the way a randomized intervention can.</p></article>
            <article><b>6</b><h3>Extreme protocols are not targets.</h3><p>A 40% deficit, extreme-volume training, bed rest, or unusually high protein intakes help test mechanisms. They are not presented as the default 12 Week Recomp prescription.</p></article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wm-final">
        <p>THE PRACTICAL CONCLUSION</p>
        <h2>Don't diet your way into a smaller, weaker version of yourself.</h2>
        <span>Use nutrition to reduce excess fat. Use resistance training and protein to protect or build lean tissue. Use cardio to strengthen the cardiovascular system. Use recovery so you can repeat the work. Judge the result by what happens to your body—not just by what happens to the scale.</span>
